# Example Caddyfile to run Alby Hub behind a Caddy reverse proxy
# Caddy has embedded letsencrypt support and creates HTTPS certificates
# learn more: https://caddyserver.com/docs/getting-started

# Refer to the Caddy docs for more information:
# https://caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile


:80 {
        # Alby Hub runs on 8029 by default (when installed as a systemd
        # service by the install script; it is 8080 when started via start.sh)
        reverse_proxy :8029
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Adding a password prompt on the web server level
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Do NOT simply wrap the block above in `basic_auth`. Alby Hub authenticates
# its own API with `Authorization: Bearer <JWT>`, and the frontend sets that
# header explicitly - which replaces the `Authorization: Basic ...` header the
# browser sends after a Basic Auth login. Pages would load, but every API call
# would be answered with 401 and the web UI would be broken.
#
# ../caddy-gate has a working configuration: Basic Auth is used for the first
# request only, after which Caddy hands out a gate cookie and leaves the
# Authorization header to Alby Hub. The install script can set it up for you.
#
# A password prompt is an extra layer, not a substitute for keeping Alby Hub
# off the public internet - prefer a VPN such as WireGuard or Tailscale.
